 been looking at to is  how can for things like quality  reporting how can we use the data that  is in there that may be coming from  these more automatic sources and just be  able to process them in a way that  provides that completes those fields  that fills in the information that you  need without someone having to go in and  do it and I think finally to having  devices that are available at point of  care they make a big difference too  because if you're relying on you know  someone's notes and then the recopying  of it there's there's a margin for error  there and then also if someone's  remembering at retaining in their head  and then going back and documenting it  later pieces of it may fall out so you  touched on participatory design when you  talked about the workshops and I know  the students have been really interested  in that in the past I wondered if you  could expand on it for us as i mentioned  i think it's it's a really useful way to  tapping into information that you might  not otherwise get because it allows  gives people the lead in terms of the  research or what they tell you and it  also i think just that it's a playful  process many times and so it enables  people to think about things in  different ways instead of am giving you  sort of a standard face-saving answer  they might come up with something that's  more creative and digs a little bit  deeper into their own emotional pool so  I think that's useful there are a  variety of different tools that people  use so the format for those of you not  familiar with it it's sort of like a  focus group only it involves people  actually engaged not just telling you  things but actually doing something with  you or for you some techniques that  people have used are called placemats so  they might give you a topic for example  tell tell me about what your work life  is like and then there's a ray of  pictures and words and other kinds of  stimuli that people could put together  and create this place mat with all this  collage of these different kinds of  things and then they go through and  explain different pieces of it that  to eliminate what they're thinking about  sometimes we give people actual or  pretend devices to work with us see if I  can get to this slide that helped them  to again to think kind of creatively and  even play act there and this one here we  gave people who were part of a clinical  team right there pretend devices so we  had like laptops we had phones we had  tablets we had all these different  things that were just constructed out of  paper but they were meant to make people  think about what devices they would use  in a particular interaction and in this  case it was about discharging people  from the hospital on how to facilitate  that so it gives people things again to  be creative with stimulate some thought  process on their part and a piece we  added to this is that they had to  play-act what the discharge would look  like if they had these other tools so we  had people who were you know pretending  to be the cell phone or talking on the  piece of cardboard and once again it  gives you a different flavor for what  what people are thinking about and how  you might go about creating a solution  so people have used a variety of  different tools for them but I think  that they're they're really useful in  terms of the hands-on and a look into  sort of the emotion and the creativity  behind things  I was interested to see your post it  notes as you're doing your opportunity  map have you explored technology  applications that might simulate that  type of posted experience particularly  for people maybe that are collaborating  remotely that's a great question we have  done some of that some white boarding  via link and other things with people  who are remote and sending messages and  things like that we've also had people  participate remotely by having them on a  video camera and and sort of sending by  instant message there there little  sticky note that we put on the main wall  but at least what i have found to date  is that there's something about that  process of having them all on the wall  and looking at them and collaborating on  how you move them together in the  clusters that i haven't found a  technological substitute for and is  similar to the you know the  brainstorming with the dot pray or  either you get five dots and yamp it i  mean there is something about the  physical interaction and kind of seeing  what other people are doing yeah we have  tried I'm doing the whiteboard thing  remotely with resounding failure because  it's hard you know other than staff  writing on it it was hard to get that  participants to contribute and I think  you have you know as much as we all try  to participate when we when we are  remote there's something about the  accountability of being in the room that  helps to focus people's attention to and  so I think that the participation and  the creativity helps it seems to work  better that way unfortunately yeah you  might be you've been doing some research  in primary care mm-hmm tell us more  about that sure that's a little older  research it's a few years old now but  what we did in that study was to shadow  primary care doctors in there in five  different cities in the US and we spent  a couple of days with each of them to  try to understand what their workflow  process was some of them were completely  electronic in terms of their  documentation some of them were still  exclusively paper but again we were  trying to understand what were some of  the constraints to adoption of  Technology  gee and how people who were the early  adopters were using what they had we  also had the opportunity as it turned  out to be at a couple practices where  people were transitioning so we got to  sort of be there with them in their pain  as they were trying to convert the paper  into electronic documentation and that  was really helpful for us to understand  really just what kind of commitment it  took for someone who was in practice for  10 or 15 years to make that transition  and try to you know get all those  documents electronically so it was one  of the things that I think we understood  from that too is how idiosyncratic the  documentation processes were in each of  those offices we had people who were  using you know either a homemade EHR or  trying to modify one that they bought  off the shelf even people who were using  paper records had different forms that  they were using we had one doctor who  had created this big rubber stamp and  put it on a piece of paper and have  these different fields that he would use  so you know it makes you makes us think  about how nimble software needs to be in  order to respond to people's needs you  have to have all this there are criteria  for what kind of what gets documented  and I think people go through medical  school and learn certain ways of  documenting data but you know beyond  that there are things that people like  to do their own way and so how can we  facilitate software do that yeah earlier  you had an image of a bunch of  clinicians gathered around particular  monitor or screen has your work inspired  the development of any collaborative  technologies and do you have any  specific examples well I know we are  working on some technologies to enable  that I'm not sure how how public the  mbox tuck is but we're working on on  some ways to help people well we're  looking at a couple of things we're  looking at leveraging existing kinds of  software that you know we might use in a  corporate setting to enable clinicians  to talk to each other but we're also  looking at some other things and where  we could embed software and devices that  would facilitate you know video  conferencing and audio conferencing and  leaving voice messages and  like that that so yeah we're trying to  comprehend that but we're still in the  works on that one I think that going  into these place